From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 10:41:48 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] storage: Read the flash of the 32-bit chips

The 32-bit devices keep their disk on two PPN NAND packages behind
Apple's VFL and SwissPPNFTL, with no NVMe in between. m1n1's main
storage on these chips is that logical disk, read-only, in the same
4 KiB blocks the boot manager reads everywhere else.

The reader is the read half of Orchard's Linux drivers (H2FMI
controller, VFL and SFTL maps), taken unchanged, so the two cannot
disagree about the format: src/arm32/sftl/README lists what comes from
where. Linux's red-black tree comes along for the map. What Linux
provides and m1n1 does not is one header and a few helpers.

The controller is used as iBoot leaves it, clocked and normally in DDR;
without that state the packages are reset and run in SDR, as the Linux
driver does. As there, only the two measured Hynix packages (64 GB and
16 GB) are accepted.

These files are GPL-2.0-only, so the 32-bit build is distributed under
the GPL; the 64-bit build does not contain them.

The translation reader is Shayne Legault's work, the controller's read
path rzxvx's (Project Cascadia) and Shayne Legault's; this commit only
carries their code over.

Compiles and links; not run. The Linux driver it is taken from has read
the whole disk of a 64 GB P105.

Co-authored-by: Shayne Legault <8741557+shaynelegault@users.noreply.github.com>
Co-authored-by: rzxvx <rrzxznewid@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: LLM
